      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the OIML certificate.\n\nThis is part 4 of 4 of certificate R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1 — a water meter certificate under OIML R49.\n\nSince this is a fragment, I should only extract what's present. But the certificate number is present, so I can fill that in. Let me look at what's here:\n\n1. Certificate number: R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\n2. Section 3.8: Table with technical and metrological data UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³, software and checksum 13 22681\n3. Section 3.9: Table with other technical and metrological data UWM\n4. Section 4: Marking and inscriptions\n5. Section 5: Trademarks (6 options)\n6. Section 6: Security measures\n7. Section 7: Figures\n\nLet me extract the characteristics.\n\n**Section 3.8 table:**\n- Water meter type: UWM\n- Nominal diameter DN: 20 mm\n- Material of body: brass / plastic\n- Permanent flowrate Q₃: 4 m³/h\n- Minimum flowrate Q₁: 20, 16, 12,7 (→12.7), 10 l/h — this is a matrix! Four variants corresponding to R values 200, 250, 315, 400.\n- Transitional flowrate Q₂: 32, 25,6 (→25.6), 20,32 (→20.32), 16 l/h — same matrix\n- Overload flowrate Q₄: 5 m³/h\n- Ratio Q₃/Q₁: R = 200, 250, 315, 400\n- Ratio Q₂/Q₁: 1,6 (→1.6)\n- Connection thread: G 1 B (mm column but it's a thread designation — value \"G 1 B\")\n- Construction length L: From 110 to 190 mm → {min: 110, max: 190}\n- Pressure loss class Δp: 0,63 bar (→0.63) / Δp63\n- Battery: 1 pc li-battery, Umin 3V, Umax 3,6V (→3.6V), life time up to 10 years\n\nSo the model variants here are defined by R (ratio Q₃/Q₁): R=200, R=250, R=315, R=400. Each has different Q1 and Q2:\n- R=200: Q1=20 l/h, Q2=32 l/h\n- R=250: Q1=16 l/h, Q2=25.6 l/h\n- R=315: Q1=12.7 l/h, Q2=20.32 l/h\n- R=400: Q1=10 l/h, Q2=16 l/h\n\nThis is a model family. Models could be named \"R=200\", \"R=250\", \"R=315\", \"R=400\" or by Q1 values. The heading says \"UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³\". So model_id could be \"UWM DN20 R200\", etc. Let me use the R ratio as the model discriminator since it directly labels the columns via the Q3/Q1 row.\n\nActually, the columns align: Q1 row has 20, 16, 12.7, 10; Q2 row has 32, 25.6, 20.32, 16; Q3/Q1 row has 200, 250, 315, 400. So:\n- R200: Q1=20, Q2=32\n- R250: Q1=16, Q2=25.6\n- R315: Q1=12.7, Q2=20.32\n- R400: Q1=10, Q2=16\n\nAlso there's the DN15 vs DN20 table at the end (Fig. 2 dimensions). But that's a figure section — the table shows dimensions for DN15 and DN20. However section 3.8 is specifically for DN20. The DN15 dimensions table is part of Fig. 2 \"Basic dimensions of the water meter UWM\". Hmm, this suggests the certificate may cover DN15 too, but the technical data table shown here is only DN20 (there may be other tables in earlier fragments for DN15). Since this is a fragment, I should extract what's here.\n\nThe dimensions table:\n- DN15: L from 110 to 190 mm, H=87, W=81, A = \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B-G3/4 B\"\n- DN20: L from 110 to 190, H=90, W=81, A=\"G1 B\"\n\nThis could be a matrix_table for dimensions. I'll include it as a matrix_table.\n\n**Section 3.9 table:**\n- Installation orientation: All positions\n- Water temperature range: \n  - 0,1 to 30 brass and plastic body (→ 0.1 to 30 °C)\n  - 0,1 to 50 brass and plastic body\n  - 0,1 to 70 brass body\n- Temperature class: T30, T50 brass and plastic body; T70 brass body\n- Maximum admissible pressure MAP: 16 bar\n- MPE in upper flowrates range Q₂ ≤ Q ≤ Q₄: ±2 (at ϑ ≤ 30°C), ±3 (at ϑ>30°C) — this varies by condition → config_level\n- MPE in lower flowrates range Q₁ ≤ Q < Q₂: ±5%\n- Capacity of calculator: 99999,99999 m³ (→ 99999.99999)\n- Scale interval: 0,00001 m³ (→ 0.00001)\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Climatic class: -25 to +55 °C (note \"- 25 to + 55\" → {min: -25, max: 55})\n- Electromagnetic class: E1 for brass body; E1, E2 for plastic body → config_level by body material\n- Climatic and mechanical environmental conditions (class): B / O (fixed meters installed in a building / outdoors)\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 D0\n\nConfig-level entries:\n- max_permissible_error_upper_range: axis \"water_temperature\": conditions \"ϑ ≤ 30°C\" → ±2%, \"ϑ > 30°C\" → ±3%\n- electromagnetic_class: axis \"body_material\": brass → \"E1\", plastic → \"E1, E2\"\n- water_temperature_range: axis \"body_material\" — hmm, but the temperature ranges also correspond to temperature classes T30/T50/T70, which are variants. The range 0.1-30 (T30, brass and plastic), 0.1-50 (T50, brass and plastic), 0.1-70 (T70, brass only). This is more of a config axis: temperature class / body material.\n\nLet me model config_level:\n```\n{\"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates_range\", \"axis\": \"water_temperature\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"ϑ ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}, {\"condition\": \"ϑ > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}]}\n```\n\nHmm, the value is ±2, which is a tolerance. I could represent as value \"±2\" or as {min: -2, max: 2}. The rule says ranges become {min, max}. ±2% means MPE of ±2%, so {min: -2, max: 2} seems reasonable. Or value 2 with meaning \"maximum permissible error magnitude\". I'll use {min: -2, max: 2}... Actually for MPE, conventionally it's expressed as ±2%, and representing as a range -2 to +2 is fine.\n\n- water_temperature_range: axis \"temperature_class\"/\"body_material\":\n  - condition \"T30, brass and plastic body\": {min: 0.1, max: 30}\n  - condition \"T50, brass and plastic body\": {min: 0.1, max: 50}\n  - condition \"T70, brass body\": {min: 0.1, max: 70}\n  unit °C\n\n- electromagnetic_class: axis \"body_material\":\n  - \"brass body\": \"E1\"\n  - \"plastic body\": \"E1, E2\"\n\nAlso pressure loss class: Δp63 with 0.63 bar.\n\nBattery: I could split into characteristics:\n- battery: {value: \"1 pc li-battery\"}\n- battery_voltage_min: 3 V, battery_voltage_max: 3.6 V — or battery_supply_voltage {min: 3, max: 3.6} V\n- battery_life_time: {min: null, max: 10} years → \"up to 10 years\"\n\nType designations: UWM, and from the photo text \"UWM-Cr3\" is mentioned (\"Ultrasonic Water Meter Model UWM-Cr3\"). Hmm, that's text visible on the meters in photos. Should I include UWM-Cr3 as a type designation? The certificate covers type UWM. The photo mentions \"Ultrasonic Water Meter Model UWM-Cr3\". It's visible in the figure. I could include it in type_designations since it's mentioned. The schema says type_designations: all type/model identifiers mentioned. I'll include \"UWM\" primarily; \"UWM-Cr3\" is visible on the meter photo — I'll include it too, as it's a model identifier mentioned.\n\nCertificate metadata:\n- number: R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\n- scheme: A (from the number pattern \"...-A-SK1-...\")\n- Recommendation: R49, edition? The number says R49/2013 — that typically means the certificate was issued under R49 edition 2013 (OIML R49-1:2013? Actually R49 has parts; 2013 edition). Hmm. The certificate number format \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" — the \"2013\" here is likely the edition year of R49 used. Actually OIML-CS numbers are like \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" where 2013 could be the year of issuance or edition. For recommendation, I'll put id: \"R49\", edition: null... Hmm. The instruction says extract recommendation id canonical e.g. \"R60\". Edition is int. R49 water meters current edition is 2013 (R49-1:2013, R49-2:2013, R49-3:2013). The \"2013\" in the cert number is ambiguous but likely the R49 edition. Given the certificate references Accuracy class 2, temperature classes, etc., R49-1:2013 edition. I'll set edition: 2013 with some caution. Hmm, but is it right? OIML-CS certificate numbers: e.g., \"R60/2000-A1-...\" Hmm. Actually OIML-CS numbering: \"OIML certificate number R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" — format is Recommendation/edition-letter scheme issuing authority-number. So 2013 is the edition of R49. I'll set edition: 2013.\n\n- revision_history: Revision 1 — revision (string \"1\"). Date not present in this fragment. Changes not present. I could include {\"revision\": \"1\"} but the schema says revision, date, changes. Don't fabricate — omit date and changes, or omit the whole entry? I'll include {\"revision\": \"1\"} only... Hmm, the fields would be null. Rule 8: don't fabricate. I'll include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"1\"}] — the certificate is \"Rev. 1\" per the header. Actually maybe safer to omit since no date/changes. But the revision marker is meaningful. I'll include it with just revision: \"1\".\n\n- page_total: 10 (from \"Page 8 of 10 pages\"). Certificate page_total: 10.\n- member_state: Slovakia (stamp: SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY). member_state: \"Slovakia\". oiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\" from the cert number. The issuer is Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM). issuing_authority: name — from the stamp \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY – X – SLM\". Hmm, the actual name might be \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" (SLM). I don't have a full name/address in this fragment. I could set issuing_authority.name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" based on the stamp. It's reasonable. But careful — rule 8: don't fabricate. The stamp literally says \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY – X – SLM\". I'll use name \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" — that's directly from the stamp. Address unknown, omit.\n\n- category: \"Water meter\" (this is an R49 water meter). The category examples list \"Gas meter\" etc. \"Water meter\" fits.\n- date_issued: not present in fragment. Omit.\n\nSoftware and checksum: \"software and checksum 13 22681\" from section 3.8 heading. That could be captured: software_checksum: \"13 22681\"? The heading says \"Table with technical and metrological data UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³, software and checksum 13 22681\". I'll record a characteristic software_checksum: {value: \"13 22681\"} or in description. Maybe characteristics.type_level: software_and_checksum: {value: \"13 22681\"}. Hmm. I'll add it as type_level characteristic \"software_checksum\" value \"13 22681\". Actually \"software and checksum 13 22681\" — could be software version and its checksum. Value \"13 22681\". I'll keep as string.\n\nConnection thread: \"G 1 B\" — value string, unit null (mm listed as unit but the value is a thread designation; the unit column says mm but value G 1 B is a thread name). I'll record connection_thread: {value: \"G 1 B\", unit_symbol: null}. Or unit \"mm\"? G 1 B is a BSP thread designation where 1 refers to 1 inch. The unit column \"mm\" seems misapplied. I'll leave unit_symbol null.\n\nFor DN15 connection thread variants: \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B-G3/4 B\" — that's in the dimensions table column A (probably connection thread). I'll put that in matrix_tables only.\n\nNow, the matrix table for flow characteristics:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data UWM DN20\",\n  \"columns\": [\"water_meter_type\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"body_material\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"connection_thread\", \"construction_length\", \"pressure_loss\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"battery\"],\n  ...\n}\n```\nHmm, that's awkward. Better: matrix over R ratio. Let me construct the table with rows per R:\n\nActually the table has mixed: single-value rows and matrix rows. For matrix_tables, I'll create a \"Flow characteristics by ratio R\" table:\ncolumns: [\"ratio_q3_q1\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q2_q1_ratio\"]\nrows:\n- {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 200, \"q1\": 20, \"q2\": 32}\n- {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 250, \"q1\": 16, \"q2\": 25.6}\n- {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 315, \"q1\": 12.7, \"q2\": 20.32}\n- {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 400, \"q1\": 10, \"q2\": 16}\nwith units: q1, q2 in l/h; q3, q4 constant (4, 5 m³/h).\n\nHmm, but q3/q4 are constant across models. In matrix_tables I can include them per row or describe them. I'll include q3: 4, q4: 5 per row for completeness (units m³/h).\n\nAnd the dimensions table:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Basic dimensions of the water meter UWM\",\n  \"columns\": [\"dn_size\", \"length_l\", \"height_h\", \"width_w\", \"connection_thread_a\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\", null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"dn_size\": \"DN15\", \"length_l\": {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 190}, \"height_h\": 87, \"width_w\": 81, \"connection_thread_a\": \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B - G 3/4 B\"},\n    {\"dn_size\": \"DN20\", \"length_l\": {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 190}, \"height_h\": 90, \"width_w\": 81, \"connection_thread_a\": \"G 1 B\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nModel family: The certificate covers UWM with DN20 (in this fragment's technical table) and possibly DN15 (dimensions). Models by R ratio: R200, R250, R315, R400. I'll create:\n\n```json\n\"model_family\": {\n  \"family_name\": \"UWM\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"UWM DN20 R200\"}, ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, actually the type designation is UWM. Models differ by R (Q3/Q1 ratio). Let me use model_id \"R=200\" etc. Or \"UWM R200\". The heading of 3.8: \"UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³\". I'll go with model_id like \"UWM DN20 R200\", \"UWM DN20 R250\", \"UWM DN20 R315\", \"UWM DN20 R400\". Simpler: \"R200\", \"R250\", \"R315\", \"R400\". I'll use \"UWM DN20 R200\" style to be explicit. Hmm, keep it simple: model_id \"R200\" etc. But schema example used \"G1.6\" style — direct size names. I'll use \"R200\" style... Let me use \"UWM R200\" — hmm. I think \"R200\" is fine and clear given attributes carry the rest.\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- minimum_flowrate_q1: unit \"l/h\", values: R200→20, R250→16, R315→12.7, R400→10\n- transitional_flowrate_q2: unit \"l/h\", values: 32, 25.6, 20.32, 16\n- ratio_q3_q1: unit \"R\", values: 200, 250, 315, 400\n\nType level characteristics (constants):\n- nominal_diameter: 20 mm — but the certificate also covers DN15? The dimensions table includes DN15. The technical data table in this fragment is only DN20. Earlier fragments (not provided) probably had DN15 table. Should nominal_diameter be model-level? Given only DN20 data table here, and the fragment heading says \"UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³\", I'll put nominal_diameter as type_level: 20 mm... but the dimensions table suggests DN15 also covered by this certificate. Hmm. Since I can't see the DN15 technical data, I'll record nominal_diameter at type level as 20 with a note? No notes. Alternatively include DN15 in models: but then model attributes (H, W) differ: DN15 H=87, DN20 H=90. Those are dimension attributes. I could make model_level entries for dn_size? That mixes.\n\nOptions:\n1. Models = R variants only (per section 3.8, the actual certified parameter set), dimensions table as matrix_table.\n2. Models = cross product DN15/DN20 × R variants — too speculative.\n\nI'll go with option 1: models are R variants for DN20 (as this fragment's data table is DN20). The dimensions matrix_table captures DN15 and DN20 dims. In type_level, I won't put nominal_diameter since it varies across the cert; actually I'll put it at type level as 20 given the heading \"UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³\" — and the dimensions table for DN15 is just Fig. 2 dims. Hmm, conflict.\n\nWait — reconsider. The certificate covers UWM type. Section 3.8 heading: \"Table with technical and metrological data UWM DN20, Q₃=4 m³, software and checksum 13 22681\". This strongly suggests earlier sections had tables for other DN sizes (DN15 etc.). Fig. 2 dimensions show DN15 and DN20. So the cert family includes DN15 and DN20 at least. For safety, I'll make nominal_diameter... hmm.\n\nGiven this is \"extract content present in THIS fragment only\", and the fragment includes both DN20 data table and a dims table covering DN15+DN20, I think the cleanest: models keyed by R ratio with model attributes DN=20, Q3=4 (from the heading, this table is DN20); and matrix_tables for both. I'll also add dn15/dn20 dimension info in matrix table only.\n\nActually, maybe better: make model_level characteristic \"nominal_diameter\" with values per model: all \"20\"? That seems redundant. Alternatively include DN in model_id: \"UWM DN20 R200\". That embeds it. I'll do that: model_id: \"DN20 R200\" etc. Hmm, \"UWM DN20 R200\" is verbose but explicit. The Q1 label: The photos show \"UWM-Cr3\" model on meter. I'll keep type_designations: [\"UWM\", \"UWM-Cr3\"]? The UWM-Cr3 is visible on the meter in the photo — \"Ultrasonic Water Meter Model UWM-Cr3\". I think including it is justified as \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". I'll include it.\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider: is \"UWM-Cr3\" really a model identifier? The photo text: \"ASM\"; barcode \"10000002\"; \"Ultrasonic Water Meter Model UWM-Cr3\"; \"CE\"; \"Q3 2.5 m³/h R=250\"... The meter shown has Q3 2.5 m³/h R=250, which differs from the DN20 table (Q3=4). So the photo shows a DN15-ish variant (Q3 2.5 m³/h R=250 would be DN15: Q3=2.5). So the family indeed includes Q3=2.5 variants. But we only have the DN20/Q3=4 table in this fragment. I'll keep type_designations [\"UWM\", \"UWM-Cr3\"] and note nothing else. Fine.\n\nTemperature class values: T30, T50, T70. Body materials: brass, plastic.\n\nLet me now write the config_level entries:\n\n1. water_temperature_range by temperature_class/body_material:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"water_temperature_range\",\n  \"axis\": \"temperature_class / body_material\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"T30, brass and plastic body\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"T50, brass and plastic body\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"T70, brass body\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n2. maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates_range by water_temperature:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates_range_q2_to_q4\",\n  \"axis\": \"water_temperature\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"ϑ ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"ϑ > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n3. electromagnetic_class by body_material:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"body_material\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"brass body\", \"value\": \"E1\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"plastic body\", \"value\": \"E1, E2\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n4. temperature_class by body_material? Could do:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"temperature_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"body_material\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"brass and plastic body\", \"value\": \"T30, T50\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"brass body\", \"value\": \"T70\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\nHmm, T30 applies to both, T50 both, T70 brass only. The table cell: \"T30, T50 brass and plastic body; T70 brass body\". So temp class list per body material. I'll include as above.\n\nAlso climatic and mechanical environmental conditions class: \"B / O\" with meaning (fixed meters installed in a building / outdoors). This is a single value with annotation. I'll record environmental_class: {value: \"B / O\", ...} hmm. Actually in R49 (based on WELMEC guidance?), class B = complete application of... Actually B and O here: \"B / O (fixed meters installed in a building / outdoors)\". I'll record as type_level: climatic_and_mechanical_environmental_class: {value: \"B / O\"} plus maybe a note. I'll keep the descriptive in description? Schema has description under certified_type. I could add the parenthetical to value? Keep value \"B / O\" and add characteristic \"environmental_conditions_note\"? Simpler: value: \"B / O (fixed meters installed in a building / outdoors)\". Strings allowed. I'll do value \"B / O\" with a companion... I'll just use the full string.\n\nHmm, careful with rule: values should be numbers where numeric, strings allowed for non-numeric. \"B / O (fixed meters installed in a building / outdoors)\" is fine as a string.\n\nType level characteristics list:\n- nominal_diameter: 20 mm (but with DN15 also... I'll include as 20 since the table says DN 20; the DN15 exists in dims table. Risky either way; I'll include type_level nominal_diameter_dn: 20 \"mm\" — and matrix table covers DN15 dims. Alternatively omit. I'll include, since 3.8 explicitly is DN20 and heading says so.)\n\nActually, let me reconsider making the model family include DN as attribute. Since dims table shows both DN15 and DN20, and 3.8's data table is DN20, maybe the intended model family from the full cert includes multiple DN sizes, but I only see DN20 data here. To be faithful: model_family.models = R variants (DN20). matrix_tables includes dims for DN15/DN20. type_level nominal_diameter: 20 mm. That's consistent within the fragment.\n\n- body_material: \"brass / plastic\" (string)\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: 4 m³/h\n- overload_flowrate_q4: 5 m³/h\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n- connection_thread: \"G 1 B\"\n- construction_length: {min: 110, max: 190} mm\n- pressure_loss: 0.63 bar\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\"\n- battery: \"1 pc li-battery\"\n- battery_voltage: {min: 3, max: 3.6} V (Umin 3V, Umax 3,6V)\n- battery_life_time: {min: null, max: 10} \"years\"\n- installation_orientation: \"All positions\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 16 bar\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrates_range_q1_to_q2: {min: -5, max: 5} % — table says \"± 5\". I'll use {min: -5, max: 5}.\n- calculator_capacity: 99999.99999 m³\n- scale_interval: 0.00001 m³\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min: -25, max: 55} °C\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\"\n- climatic_and_mechanical_environmental_conditions_class: \"B / O\"\n- software_checksum: \"13 22681\"\n\nAlso \"software and checksum 13 22681\" — I'll name it software_and_checksum? Better: software_checksum: \"13 22681\". Hmm, could be software version \"13\" and checksum \"22681\"? Ambiguous. Keep raw string value \"13 22681\" under software_and_checksum.\n\nBattery life: \"life time up to 10 years\" → value {min: null, max: 10}, unit \"years\".\n\nPressure loss class row: \"0,63<br>Δp63\" with unit \"bar<br>-\": so pressure loss 0.63 bar and class Δp63. Good.\n\nMatrix tables: I'll include two:\n1. \"Technical and metrological data UWM DN20 by ratio Q3/Q1\" — rows per R.\nActually since model_level already captures q1/q2/ratio per model, do I need this matrix_table? The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. This one fits into model_level, so it's captured. But the R137 example shows extracting the explicit 2-D table as matrix_tables AND populating model_level. I'll follow that: include the matrix table too.\n\nmatrix_table 1:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data UWM DN20 (flow characteristics by ratio Q3/Q1)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"ratio_q3_q1\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q2_over_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [\"R\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"l/h\", \"l/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 200, \"q3\": 4, \"q4\": 5, \"q1\": 20, \"q2\": 32, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6},\n    {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 250, \"q3\": 4, \"q4\": 5, \"q1\": 16, \"q2\": 25.6, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6},\n    {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 315, \"q3\": 4, \"q4\": 5, \"q1\": 12.7, \"q2\": 20.32, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6},\n    {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 400, \"q3\": 4, \"q4\": 5, \"q1\": 10, \"q2\": 16, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Water meter type UWM, DN20, Q3 = 4 m³/h, software and checksum 13 22681. Values converted from decimal comma.\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n```\n\nmatrix_table 2 (dimensions):\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Basic dimensions of the water meter UWM (Fig. 2)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"dn_size\", \"length_l\", \"height_h\", \"width_w\", \"connection_thread\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\", null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"dn_size\": \"DN15\", \"length_l\": {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 190}, \"height_h\": 87, \"width_w\": 81, \"connection_thread\": \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B - G 3/4 B\"},\n    {\"dn_size\": \"DN20\", \"length_l\": {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 190}, \"height_h\": 90, \"width_w\": 81, \"connect